
NOTEs / trade offs:

- "eventual consistency": never a "consistent" snapshot

- html.parser.HTMLParser subclass, hrefs only │ 3-5ms vs BeautifulSoup + html.parser │ 50-100ms
    > not building a full DOM to cap cpu load

    parser reliability:
    4. html.parser is lenient, not spec-compliant. On malformed markup it can skip tags lxml would catch.
    5. JS-loaded content. Anything not in the served HTML is invisible. Collapsed navboxes are fine (present, just hidden);
    > html parsing is not always an "exact science". Stick to html.parser for simplicity of the context
    > filtering out category pages bc they are giant hubs ("article links" from pdf)
        > by section id="mw-content-text", and by name prefix within the content div

- wikiparse-rs for sql->sqlite // wikiwalk / wiki-graph — full solutions, avoid
    - did not look at wikiwalk OO design to build my own interpretation

- production-ready, so the progress is stored on db
    - if a link is in a page on db but not fetched yet, fetch+process
        if it's a red link, do not retry for 24 hours

        Simple walking, no lookahead within batch/chunk, or across chunks
        All links we do not have are fetched in background, starting at the beginning of the chunk
            The first one not yet fetched/processed blocks the loop.


- db + Walker supports red links


